<Article
  title="Aura Farms: salad that grows up the wall"
  description="Return to Aura. One beautiful room at a time."
  kicker="Farms"
  date="2026-09-30"
>
  <p>Aeroponics grows plants with their roots hanging in the air, misted with water and nutrients instead of planted in soil. In an Aura grow room, the garden is a wall: vertical channels along one long side, with grow lights for when the sun clocks out.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">Why a wall, not a greenhouse beside it</RevealHeading>
  <p>The room is already sealed, glazed and ready for the land. Putting the garden in the wall keeps your harvest in a room you can lock, heat and move. The other long side stays polycarbonate, so the view is still the view. It is the same <a href="/#shell">Aura room</a>, with a greener personality.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">Who it's for</RevealHeading>
  <p>Grow your own for the house, the kitchen or a small stay. Or have Aura build the room and set it up where the food is needed. Either way, it is a room with a garden built in. It is not a franchise, a produce brand or a promise of a set harvest.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">What it can grow</RevealHeading>
  <p>Leafy greens and herbs are the easy first crop. Fruit can work too, it just takes longer. How much you pick depends on light, temperature and how often someone is around to harvest, so we do not put numbers on a web page.</p>
  <p>The bigger plan for grow rooms is in <a href="/journal/free-food-and-water">the Aura Network note</a>. The room itself is on <a href="/farms">Aura Farms</a>.</p>
</Article>
